{"moat":{"id":"f4f5d526-80ce-45e2-b2b4-edbba2fd8dc0","word":"moat","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"defensive ditch","tags":["masculine"],"word":"fosso"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"wikidata":["Q88480"],"senseid":["en:Q88480"],"links":[["defensive","defensive"],["ditch","ditch"],["water","water"],["fortified","fortified"],["habitation","habitation"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"fosse"}],"glosses":["A deep, wide defensive ditch, normally filled with water, surrounding a fortified habitation."],"id":"bf80fad8-4627-41e7-868f-211c2f24ce44"},{"examples":[{"text":"No matter how good your company's product is or how quickly the industry is growing, if there is no moat, competitors will invade your castle and burn it down.","ref":"2013, John Mauldin, Jonathan Tepper, Code Red: How to Protect Your Savings From the Coming Crisis, John Wiley & Sons, →ISBN:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[100,104]],"id":"e1409241-b26f-4b85-982f-3d4d49638e0b"},{"text":"“I think ‘moats’ are lame,” Mr. Musk had said during a Tesla earnings call. It was a criticism of an economic principle that Mr. Buffett had coined in 1999 and that has become something of a mantra for his faithful: Invest in businesses “that have wide, sustainable moats around them.”","ref":"2018 May 7, Andrew Ross Sorkin, quoting Elon Musk, “Elon Musk Wants to Fill Warren Buffett’s ‘Moat’ With Candy, but It Still Holds Water”, in The New York Times, →ISSN:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[10,15],[266,271]],"id":"f486ca50-851b-4227-b998-11a2858d182c"},{"text":"Moreover, where older firms might have to stave off competitors and engage in practices like predatory pricing to keep the competition at bay, platform monopolies have a built-in moat. Since a platform’s value comes from its number of users—a phenomenon known as the network effect—competing against large platforms is nigh impossible since its monopoly position makes it inherently more useful.","ref":"2023 November 28, JS Tan, “It's All Bullshit”, in The Baffler:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[179,183]],"id":"643bb111-fbcf-4269-8ccf-1449a1d4430d"}],"topics":["business"],"categories":["English terms with quotations","en:Business"],"links":[["business","business"]],"raw_glosses":["(business, figurative) An aspect of a business which makes it more \"defensible\" from competitors, because of the nature of its products, services or franchise or for some other reason."],"glosses":["An aspect of a business which makes it more \"defensible\" from competitors, because of the nature of its products, services or franchise or for some other reason."],"tags":["figuratively"],"id":"4af11cfc-6aac-4979-9303-6f79613a44a0"},{"links":[["lowland","lowland"],["caldera","caldera"]],"glosses":["A circular lowland between a resurgent dome and the walls of the caldera surrounding it."],"id":"fea756d1-9b73-4029-8304-cbb40b9eee77"},{"topics":["climatology","meteorology","natural-sciences"],"categories":["en:Meteorology"],"links":[["meteorology","meteorology"],["eyewall","eyewall"],["tropical cyclone","tropical cyclone"]],"raw_glosses":["(meteorology) A clear ring outside the eyewall of a tropical cyclone."],"glosses":["A clear ring outside the eyewall of a tropical cyclone."],"id":"256419aa-3905-4e78-8b9b-42be2e752fb1"},{"categories":["English terms with obsolete senses"],"links":[["hill","hill"],["mound","mound"]],"raw_glosses":["(obsolete) A hill or mound."],"glosses":["A hill or mound."],"tags":["obsolete"],"id":"1295f788-bffb-4af5-baef-98c6ba69db22"}],"related":[{"word":"motte"},{"word":"cunette"}],"synonyms":[]},"moa":{"id":"1c9203af-284e-499a-8085-b6ccf5086101","word":"moa","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"extinct bird","word":"moa"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"The moas (order Dinornithiformes) of New Zealand are likewise extinct but almost certainly some still survived when Tasman first sighted the islands in 1642.","ref":"2000, Errol Fuller, Extinct Birds, Oxford, page 29:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[4,8]],"id":"1176e8d5-662c-4770-814c-c57f4a884e2b"},{"text":"For instance, the little bush moa fed nearly exclusively on forest vegetation, whereas the heavy-footed moa was a grazer in open vegetation habitats.","ref":"2018 April 4, Hanneke Meijer, The Guardian, archived from the original on 04 Apr 2018:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[30,33],[104,107]],"id":"e212283e-c156-4993-b353-c9a419578b6d"}],"attestations":[{"date":"from 19th c.","references":[]}],"links":[["extinct","extinct"],["flightless","flightless"],["bird","bird"],["Dinornithidae","Dinornithidae#Translingual"],["New Zealand","New Zealand"]],"categories":["English terms with quotations","Quotation templates to be cleaned"],"glosses":["Any of several species of large, extinct, flightless birds of the family Dinornithidae that were native to New Zealand; until its extinction, one species was the largest bird in the world."],"id":"9bfc44d9-d76e-48ba-8563-c37a8b41727f"},{"categories":["English internet slang","English terms with uncommon senses"],"links":[["Internet","Internet"],["extremely","extremely"],["tall","tall"],["individual","individual"]],"raw_glosses":["(Internet slang, uncommon) An extremely tall individual."],"glosses":["An extremely tall individual."],"tags":["Internet","uncommon"],"id":"dd407e59-c21f-4877-8afd-dfbe63e9c3ce"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"moai":{"id":"dcbb4053-98a8-4fce-83d1-142db5fde8aa","word":"moai","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"stone statue on Easter Island","tags":["masculine"],"word":"moai"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"As the years passed, the Moais and Ahus became larger and larger, many with red scoria top crowns weighing as much as ten tons.","ref":"2012, John Loret, John T. Tanacredi, Easter Island, Springer Science & Business Media, →ISBN, page 26:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[25,30]],"id":"d3e1d87f-1cc1-4ccb-b071-3c99a7c1e082"}],"links":[["stone","stone"],["statue","statue"],["Easter Island","Easter Island"],["Rapa Nui","Rapa Nui"]],"categories":["English countable nouns","English entries with incorrect language header","English indeclinable nouns","English lemmas","English nouns","English nouns with irregular plurals","English terms borrowed from Rapa Nui","English terms derived from Rapa Nui","English terms with quotations","Entries with translation boxes","Pages with 5 entries","Pages with entries","Terms with Bulgarian translations","Terms with Catalan translations","Terms with Cherokee translations","Terms with Finnish translations","Terms with German translations","Terms with Japanese translations","Terms with Korean translations","Terms with Mandarin translations","Terms with Portuguese translations","Terms with Spanish translations","Terms with Swedish translations"],"glosses":["One of the large stone statues on Easter Island (Rapa Nui)."],"id":"f418366f-8d44-4f21-ac80-62949431cec7"}],"related":[{"word":"🗿"},{"english":"the stone platform of the moai","translation":"the stone platform of the moai","word":"ahu"}],"synonyms":[]},"moan":{"id":"0365cc1b-10a5-4dcc-a4cc-e490a4cc0ff2","word":"moan","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"to complain","word":"queixar-se"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"to complain","word":"reclamar"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"to make a moan or similar sound","word":"gemer"}],"pos":"verb","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Much did the Craven seeme to mone his case […].","ref":"1596, Edmund Spenser, “Book VI, Canto VII”, in The Faerie Queene. […], London: […] [John Wolfe] for William Ponsonbie, →OCLC:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[29,33]],"id":"5b59a37f-1d88-41f1-85fb-eeda96cb603f"},{"text":"1708, Matthew Prior, the Turtle and the Sparrow\nYe floods, ye woods, ye echoes, moan / My dear Columbo, dead and gone.","type":"quotation","id":"c08d88dc-9ac8-44c2-b5a0-60a7fc9e1828"}],"attestations":[{"date":"from 13th c.","references":[]}],"categories":["British English","English terms with quotations","English transitive verbs"],"links":[["bemoan","bemoan"],["bewail","bewail"],["mourn","mourn"]],"raw_glosses":["(transitive, chiefly UK) To complain about; to bemoan, to bewail; to mourn."],"glosses":["To complain about; to bemoan, to bewail; to mourn."],"tags":["UK","transitive"],"id":"4536bd67-60b2-48be-ae86-e79b55687d8c"},{"attestations":[{"date":"from 14th c.","references":[]}],"categories":["English intransitive verbs","English poetic terms"],"links":[["grieve","grieve"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"lament"},{"word":"aggrieve","source":"Thesaurus:be sad","tags":["obsolete"]},{"word":"besorrow","source":"Thesaurus:be sad"},{"word":"condole","source":"Thesaurus:be sad","tags":["obsolete"]},{"word":"darken","source":"Thesaurus:be sad"},{"word":"engrieve","source":"Thesaurus:be sad","tags":["archaic"]},{"word":"erme","source":"Thesaurus:be sad","tags":["obsolete"]},{"word":"gloom","source":"Thesaurus:be sad"},{"word":"grieve","source":"Thesaurus:be sad"},{"word":"grieven","source":"Thesaurus:be sad"},{"word":"bleed","source":"Thesaurus:be sad"},{"word":"lament","source":"Thesaurus:be sad"},{"word":"moan","source":"Thesaurus:be sad","tags":["poetic"]},{"word":"mourn","source":"Thesaurus:be sad"},{"word":"remorse","source":"Thesaurus:be sad"},{"word":"repent","source":"Thesaurus:be sad"},{"word":"regret","source":"Thesaurus:be sad"},{"word":"rue","source":"Thesaurus:be sad","tags":["archaic"]},{"word":"sigh","source":"Thesaurus:be sad"},{"word":"sorrow","source":"Thesaurus:be sad"},{"word":"wayment","source":"Thesaurus:be sad","tags":["obsolete"]},{"word":"yearn","source":"Thesaurus:be sad","tags":["obsolete"]}],"raw_glosses":["(intransitive, now chiefly poetic) To grieve."],"glosses":["To grieve."],"tags":["intransitive","poetic"],"id":"c2a47e0c-5372-4293-ad96-49a7ce193cea"},{"examples":[{"text":"She moaned with pleasure and squirmed with delight from receiving oral sex.","bold_text_offsets":[[4,10]],"type":"example","id":"34c3d373-3302-4a15-b3ef-8c8856d8da80"},{"text":"They shared a common dread that he would begin moaning.","ref":"1961 November 10, Joseph Heller, “The Soldier in White”, in Catch-22 […], New York, N.Y.: Simon and Schuster, →OCLC, page 169:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[47,54]],"id":"de464231-50ed-402c-9cc7-7ceec412f350"}],"attestations":[{"date":"from 18th c.","references":[]}],"categories":["English intransitive verbs","English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples"],"links":[["sound","sound"]],"raw_glosses":["(intransitive) To make a moan or similar sound."],"glosses":["To make a moan or similar sound."],"tags":["intransitive"],"id":"dc0f4b71-f7a4-46cb-a282-183cdeb053bf"},{"examples":[{"text":"‘Please don't leave me,’ he moaned.","bold_text_offsets":[[28,34]],"type":"example","id":"8266f8ef-8883-444b-bec2-ffed32bb1c23"}],"attestations":[{"date":"from 19th c.","references":[]}],"categories":["English terms with usage examples","English transitive verbs"],"links":[["say","say"]],"raw_glosses":["(transitive) To say in a moan, or with a moaning voice."],"glosses":["To say in a moan, or with a moaning voice."],"tags":["transitive"],"id":"0ae7af8f-1130-4a65-8350-42cd55e9fbe2"},{"attestations":[{"date":"from 20th c.","references":[]}],"categories":["English colloquialisms","English intransitive verbs"],"links":[["complain","complain"],["grumble","grumble"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"whine"},{"word":"beef","source":"Thesaurus:complain"},{"word":"bellyache","source":"Thesaurus:complain"},{"word":"bemoan","source":"Thesaurus:complain"},{"word":"bitch","source":"Thesaurus:complain"},{"word":"bleat","source":"Thesaurus:complain"},{"word":"whimper","source":"Thesaurus:complain"},{"word":"carp","source":"Thesaurus:complain"},{"word":"chunter","source":"Thesaurus:complain"},{"word":"complain","source":"Thesaurus:co